INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('kitab-al-riyad', 1, 'The Perfect and the Perfection of the Soul', 'The opening of *Kitab al-Riyad* in full: the damaged invocation that survives, al-Kirmani''s preface and prayer for the ruling Imam, his statement of method, the table of ten chapters, and the first ten sub-chapters of Chapter One — where the foundational doctrine of the book (perfect in itself, deficient only in relation) is established against al-Sijistani''s persistent attempt to fix the Soul on a single rank-scale that would make it deficient in itself.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('kitab-al-riyad', 2, 'Soul, Intellect, and the Power of Emanation', 'The middle stretch of Chapter One of *Kitab al-Riyad* — sub-chapters eleven through twenty-three — where al-Sijistani exhausts his attacks on the perfection of the Soul through a parade of analogies, and al-Kirmani holds the formula "perfect in itself, deficient only in relation" at every turn. The Two Weighty Things hadith arrives at the structural center; the dolphins of the Sea of Oman arrive near the end. The episode closes at the threshold of the sperm-and-man analogy that opens episode five.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('kitab-al-riyad', 3, 'The Soul in Time and the Rejoinder to al-Nusra', 'The closing stretch of Chapter One of *Kitab al-Riyad* — sub-chapters twenty-four through thirty-eight. Al-Sijistani''s last lines of attack (the sperm-and-man analogy, the Speaker-and-Foundation ranking, the male-female pair, the souls subject to nature that forget their world, the fourfold division of objects/idea/words/writing, the Intellect-and- time-together claim) meet al-Kirmani''s decisive distinction between the universal Soul and the sensual soul of struggling human beings. The chapter ends on the prayer that seals the whole argument and on the bridge to Chapter Two.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('kitab-al-riyad', 5, 'The Intellect as the First Creation', 'Nine sub-chapters of the second chapter of *Kitab al-Riyad* — al-Kirmani''s narrow, dense argument that the First and Second Intellects are not bodies, and that the whole vocabulary of motion, stillness, need, dwelling, and encompassing therefore does not apply to them. The chapter answers two competing wrong pictures (al-Razi''s "motion and stillness united in the First and the Second" and al-Sijistani''s "motion for the Soul, stillness for the Intellect") with the same correction: the categories of bodies do not transfer to the world of origination. The structural climax is the overflow doctrine: the *hudud* withhold nothing; the limitation is in the receiver''s capacity. The Quranic anchors (17:31 and 4:6) carry the doctrinal weight. The chapter ends on the warning that confusion about the ranks of the *hudud* corrupts the essence of the souls that try to perceive without that discipline.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('kitab-al-riyad', 6, 'Soul and Spirit — One Substance or Two?', 'Six sub-chapters of *Chapter Three* of *Kitab al-Riyad* — al-Kirmani''s judgment among the *hudud* on whether the Soul (the Second) and the lower soul (*al-Hayuli*, prime matter) are similar to the First or not. The chapter answers: the First and the Second are like-counterparts, the mineral of honor, perfection, and elevation; *al-Hayuli* is the trace of the Second, not its child; the higher and lower worlds do not need to resemble each other to be in contact, because the contact is the work of the power learned from the First, running through the Second, impressing the substrate. Al-Nusra''s body-categories — sides, distance, diminution — are refused one by one. The closing sub-chapter accepts al-Nusra''s final reconstruction of the chain (Intellect → Soul → nature) once he has dropped the body-talk. The doctrinal payload sets the architecture for the rest of *al-Riyad*: the *hudud* are linked to their principles by office, not by likeness.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('kitab-al-riyad', 7, 'Souls — Parts of the First Truths, or Only Traces?', 'Eight sub-chapters of *Chapter Four* of *Kitab al-Riyad* — al-Kirmani''s adjudication of the longest-running disagreement between the two reformers: are the speaking souls in us *parts* of the First Truths (the universal Second) or only *traces* of them? The chapter walks the dispute through eight passes. Sub-chapter one reads both reformers as half-right: al-Islah''s "traces" protected the soul from body-status; al-Nusra''s "parts" protected the soul''s continuity with what stands above. Sub-chapters two and three set up and refuse al-Nusra''s "parts of the total soul" reading by a precise dilemma. Sub-chapter four delivers the doctrinal hinge: the speaking souls are the *second emanation*, the second source. Sub-chapters five and six work the unlimited-capacity proof of non-embodiment and grant only a restricted parts-by-rank language. Sub-chapter seven is the polemical climax: prophethood, imamate, walaya, and the divine laws do not stand or fall by the parts-claim at all; they rest on the rank attained by the prophet''s soul through the chain of obedience and worship, on the *hudud* that convey the gift. Sub-chapter eight delivers the deepest move: the Intellect is nothing but the Word, the Word is nothing but the Intellect — the formula Imam al-Mu''izz li-Din Allah taught in *Ta''wil al-Shari''a*. The doctrinal payload sets the architecture for the rest of *al-Riyad*: the *hudud* convey; the souls receive; the chain holds by the running of the single originating power, not by any substance-composition between ranks.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('kitab-al-riyad', 8, 'The Human Being — Fruit of All the Worlds', 'Seven sub-chapters of *Chapter Five* of *Kitab al-Riyad* — al-Kirmani''s adjudication of the next great dispute between the two reformers: is the human being the *fruit* of this world (the harvest of the long natural ascent from elements into the form prepared to receive the three souls), as the author of *al-Islah* said, or the fruit of the *higher* world (the abode of survival to which the prophets called us), as the author of *al-Nusra* insisted? Sub-chapter one frames the dispute and lands the third word: the human being is the *fruit of all the worlds* — first perfection from the natural world, second perfection from the higher world, on the authority of Imam al-Mu''izz li-Din Allah''s *Ta''wil al-Shari''a*. Sub-chapter two turns *al-Nusra''s* "would-not-move" objection back on him and gives the structural formula: human beings are formed by *the meeting of the overflow of the worlds*. Sub-chapter three refuses *al-Islah''s* extension of emanation to *al-Hayula* AND *al-Nusra''s* perfect-and-deficient marriage analogy, settling the doctrine that each rank is perfect in itself and deficient only when added to another. Sub-chapter four settles the name *al-Tamamiyyah* on the Creator. Sub-chapter five settles the synonymy of the divine names (cause, effect, perfect, perfected) *according to the additions* — one named, multiple aspects, the same architecture *Chapter Four* settled for Intellect-and-Word. Sub-chapter six delivers the chapter''s deepest move: the universal Soul *did not seek* in her action; God enriched her at origination; her overflow is the work of giving, not of getting. From that, the chapter''s signature formula: *human beings have two sides — one part natural, one part divine; the last in the universes, united in eternity with what has been acquired by time*. Sub-chapter seven closes on the First Creator''s metaphorical action and the Intellect that perceives only the truth. The doctrinal payload sets the architecture for *Chapter Six''s* coming question of motion, stillness, *al-Hayula*, and form: the human being is the rank at which the chain of emanation from above meets the chain of natural cause-and-effect from below; the *hudud* convey the gift; the believer who knows the houses of the Prophets, the guardians, the imams, and the righteous is ready for salvation, gathered in *Dar al-Quds*.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('kitab-al-riyad', 9, 'Motion, Stillness, *al-Hayula*, and Form', 'Nine sub-chapters of *Chapter Six* of *Kitab al-Riyad* — al-Kirmani''s adjudication of the next argument the two reformers handed down: what are motion, stillness, *al-hayula* (prime matter), and form, and what kind of analogy from soul-and-body, if any, can honestly capture them? The author of *al-Islah* had offered a gentler-and-denser proportion: motion-and-stillness as the *soul* of *al-hayula*-and-form. The author of *al-Nusra* had refused the proportion and offered a structural parallel keyed to division and similarity of parts. Sub-chapter one frames the dispute and lands the chapter''s structural doctrine: motion and stillness are *opposites that do not meet in the same place at the same time*; form and *al-hayula* are *inseparable correlates, actor and acted-upon*. Sub-chapter two presses the consequence — *al-Islah''s* proportion would force the soul to be a body, which it is not. Sub-chapter three handles *al-Nusra''s* structural parallel and settles the synonymy of *al-nafs* and *al-ruh* as one non-bodily reality. Sub-chapter four delivers the positive doctrine of motion (*that by which the same thing changes*) and the architectural map (form-and-*al-hayula* as *the first emanation*). Sub-chapter five handles *al-Islah''s* analogy from *kun* and refuses it *as an image*. Sub-chapter six handles the related doctrine of two proportions and qualifies the analogy *by first-and-accident*. Sub-chapter seven is the chapter''s hinge — the *grave error* of *pre-creative creativity*, refused categorically in the name of *tawhid*: *the Creative IS the creativity*. Sub-chapter eight seals the doctrine at the First Effect: above it, no motion, no rest, no body-talk whatsoever. Sub-chapter nine closes on what contradictions among bodies actually are — not in their being bodies, but in *the images within them*. The doctrinal payload sets the architecture for *Chapter Seven''s* coming question of *the sections of the world*: the body''s differentiation is at the form-level, the image-level, what *al-hayula* has received; the world''s architectural divisions follow the proportions of receivership at each rank, the same architecture *Chapter Four* settled for the souls and *Chapter Five* settled for the human being.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('kitab-al-riyad', 10, 'The Sections of the World', 'Seven sub-chapters of *Chapter Seven* of *Kitab al-Riyad* — al-Kirmani''s adjudication of *the sections of the world*: what the world''s actual divisions are, where the human being sits among those sections, what kind of *pair* the human has, and on what footing the doctrine of the microcosm can honestly stand. The author of *al-Islah* had offered a clean four-jewels-four- structures-four-classes scheme with the horse paired to humankind by essence-sharing and humankind itself as the counterpart of the whole world with no peer among its parts. The author of *al-Nusra* had refused the four-classes division and offered a pairing-by- acceptance scheme: the dense lower world as pair of the subtle upper world; the horse as humankind''s pair because the horse accepts teaching from humankind more than other animals do. Sub- chapter one frames the dispute and lands the architectural correction (the world has many more sections than four; the generated beings are three genera plus human-by-distinction). Sub-chapter two refuses the no-peer claim from two angles and lands the pairing-by-occasion doctrine (angels, lions, wolves, crows, tigers, monkeys, depending on what the human has chosen to resemble). Sub-chapter three settles the strict pair of *strict- sense humankind* as the angels and names the *walaya* chain that constitutes the strict-sense humankind. Sub-chapter four turns to Bandhaqlis (Empedocles) on the bestial soul and speaking soul, refuses *al-Nusra*''s literal reading, and gives the *ta''wil* (teachers and receivers, not horse-and-human), with Q 89:27–30 as the Qur''anic anchor. Sub-chapter five settles the architectural map of the microcosm in its disputed form (*al-Islah*''s body-to- bodies-growth-to-plants-sense-to-animals-speaking-soul-as-unique- fourth preferred to *al-Nusra*''s re-mapping). Sub-chapter six fills the map at full breadth and adds the creative-emanative world parallels (intellect, praise, *tawhid*, virtue), pointing the reader to *Rahat al-''Aql*. Sub-chapter seven lands the *between*-state doctrine: the human is the child of *both worlds*, matter from below and image from above, the parents in the highest picture being the universal Intellect and the universal Soul, the midwife who delivers being the *hudud of the da''wa*. The doctrinal payload sets the architecture for *Chapter Eight*''s coming question of *al-qada and al-qadar* (judgment and destiny) — how the chain runs in time.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('kitab-al-riyad', 11, 'Qada and Qadar — Fate and Destiny', 'Twenty-four sub-chapters of *Chapter Eight* of *Kitab al-Riyad* — al-Kirmani''s adjudication of *al-qada* (judgment, decree) and *al-qadar* (destiny, measure) — what they mean, which is prior, which is posterior, and what the order of the chain that runs in time looks like at every rank. The author of *al-Islah* had argued that destiny precedes decree because *destiny is estimation and decree is detail* — like a tailor estimating a garment before he cuts it. The author of *al-Nusra* had inverted the order: decree precedes destiny, like the *spiritual image of the sewing* in the tailor''s soul precedes the appreciation by which he prepares to cut. Sub-chapters one and two frame the opening dispute on the definitions and the two tailor-analogies, and land al-Kirmani''s first move (the dispute cannot stand on the bodily reading alone). Sub-chapters three through five give the three meanings of *qada* (the speaker-Prophet, the bringing-out by *ta''wil*, the metaphysical fixity of the world''s laws). Sub-chapters six through eight give the three meanings of *qadar* (the promised *Qa''im*, the revelation and Shariʿah, the world unfolding in time), with Quran 70:6–7 as the Qur''anic anchor. Sub-chapter nine corrects the doctrine of the First Intellect as *the assembly of the images of the worlds*. Sub-chapters ten through fifteen work Quran 54:49 (*Indeed, We have created everything according to a measure*) and give the *Pen-Tablet-Writer* architecture. Sub-chapters sixteen through nineteen work the Prophetic word *Flee from Allah''s qada to His qadar* in three meanings, with Quran 18:77 as al-Islah''s anchor. Sub-chapter twenty gives the political *ta''wil* of Moses asking Shuʿayb for the agency to lift the affliction of the people of truth at the wrong time (Quran 7:143 and Quran 41:53). Sub-chapters twenty-one and twenty-two refuse the doctrine *the mind is all things and all things are the mind*. Sub-chapter twenty-three refuses *al-Nusra*''s reading of Quran 5:93 as a license to abandon the Shariʿah and gives the verse a different *ta''wil* (a license to the *du''at* to study outside sciences as a weapon under three conditions). Sub-chapter twenty-four lands the three-position order: *decree at the beginning, apportioning in the middle, completion at the end* — anchored to Quran 89:27–30 and the *reassured soul* that *returns to its Lord, pleased and pleasing*. The doctrinal payload sets the architecture for *Chapter Nine*''s coming question of *the law of Adam and the wilayah of Nuh* — how the chain that runs in time first establishes itself at the head of human history.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('kitab-al-riyad', 12, 'The Shariʿah of Adam and the First Speaker', 'The first nineteen sub-chapters of *Chapter Nine* of *Kitab al-Riyad* — al-Kirmani''s adjudication of whether Adam (peace be upon him) had a *Shariʿah*. The author of *al-Nusra* presses that he did not — the *qurban* (offering) verse is a parable, *one of Adam''s sons did not know how to bury his brother until Allah sent a raven*, the verse of *Shariʿah* (Quran 42:13) names Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, and Muhammad ﷺ but not Adam, the angels are not assigned and are not obligated, and *if Adam had been the owner of a Shariʿah, Allah would have commanded the angels to prostrate themselves through him*. The author of *al-Islah* had affirmed the opposite — the *qurban* settles it on its apparent reading, the universally affirmative verses settle it independently, and the *speaker* cannot be a speaker without a *Shariʿah* drawn among his nation. Al-Kirmani works the dispute point by point: the *qurban* on apparent + the deep *ta''wil* of *the two sons* as *wali* and *opposite* and the *raven* as *Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr*; the *narrative tradition* naming Adam as *prescriber of the rites of the dead*; the *prostration of the angels* establishing Adam''s *Mustafa*-rank; the *parallel verse to Israel* showing that *silent intermediaries* are not *no intermediaries*; the *natural-world angels* distinguished from the *abode-of-eternity high limits*; the *roster argument* refuted by the parallel roster of Quran 4:163; the *three souls* and the *six gates of necessity* constitutive of *human-being-as-such*; the *Noah-parallel* showing *violation* presupposes *prior limits*; the *tent at the qibla* and Seth as *first asas*; the refusal of the *bare-world-sufficient-for- tawhid* doctrine. *The Shariʿah of Adam (peace be upon him) is fixed.* The chapter bridges to its own second half — the *prophets-as-teachers* doctrine, the *monotheism-and-the-ranks* architecture, and the *wilayah of Noah*.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('kitab-al-riyad', 13, 'Prophets as Teachers, Monotheism, and the Ranks', 'The second half of *Chapter Nine* of *Kitab al-Riyad* — fourteen sub-chapters in which al-Kirmani takes the verdict of the first half (*Adam had a Shariʿah, and Seth was his asas*) and *expands* it into the architectural shape of the chain that runs through human history. Sub-chapter twenty: *the work is for soul- correction, not only for tawhid*; the deep reading of *I know what you do not know* (Quran 2:30) on the architecture of *force and act* across the cycles. Sub-chapters twenty-one and twenty-two: distinguishing the *interregnum role* of the Qa''im (knowledge widespread) from the *post-Qa''im role* (door of education closed). Sub-chapter twenty-three: the *three sections of Adam''s call* — encouragement, intimidation, or both — each requiring *parable* and therefore *apparent meaning followed by interpretation*. Sub-chapter twenty-four: *knowledge and action together* as the *one Shariʿah* — *body and spirit of the religion*. Sub-chapter twenty-five: *the Qa''im completes, does not abrogate* — the apocalypse- verses concern *the passing of the ranks*, not the *laws and ordinances of works*. Sub-chapter twenty-six: *the high limits attain perfection from innovation*; the *bodily intellects* from *acquisition through teaching*. Sub-chapters twenty-seven and twenty-eight: the *seven lines and six aradas* dispute — al-Islah''s reading corrected, al-Nusra''s refused, al-Kirmani''s positive geometry of the speaker-cycles. Sub-chapter twenty-nine: *As We began the first creation, We shall repeat it* (Quran 21:104) on the *cyclic return*. Sub-chapter thirty: the *Adam-letters* argument refuted by internal inconsistency. Sub-chapters thirty-one through thirty-three: the *ark of Noah* with both readings sound (apparent Shariʿah + daʿwa-with-the-wali), the doctrine that *every speaker has a guardian* (Adam had Seth), and the deep *taʾwil* of *the son of Noah* as the *opposite* who is washed away in the cleansing flood that spreads *outwardly and inwardly* — the type that recurs at every cycle until the seventh. Bridge to *Chapter Ten*: *Tawhid and the critique of al-Mahsul* — the doctrine of the First Creator above the head of the chain.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('kitab-al-riyad', 14, 'Tawhid and the Critique of al-Mahsul', 'Sixteen sub-chapters of *Chapter Ten* of *Kitab al-Riyad* — the book''s closing critique. Al-Kirmani turns from the architecture of the chain to the rank *above* the chain — *tawhid* (taw-heed — monotheism) and *al-mubdi al-awwal* (the First Creator). The author of *al-Mahsul* had written a chapter on the *first thing in what was first to fix*: tawhid and the First Creator. Abu Hatim al-Razi, in *al-Islah*, had passed over the errors. Al-Kirmani enters at the close of his own treatise — having promised the work at the book''s beginning — to *discharge the right of religion by stating what is implicated in al-Mahsul''s chapter and what it is not permissible to believe*. Sub-chapter one frames the doctrine of *cooperation in the sciences of religion like the senses*. Sub-chapter two lands the *threefold impossibility* against the *Creator of the thing — and what is not under these elements*. Sub-chapters three through eight work the *grammar of nothing* and refuse al-Mahsul''s *He and no thing with Him* (with the verse of self-witness Q 3:18, the verse of incomparability Q 42:11, and the Cairo-court doctrine of *huwa* as the only pronoun *positive for what is outside of it, without any attribute or plurality*, anchored to Imam al-Mu''izz li-Din Allah''s *Ta''wil al-Shara''i''*). Sub-chapter nine corrects al- Mahsul''s confusion of the *acquired intellect* (which gathers the images of both worlds) with the *First Intellect* (which is *self- knowing alone*). Sub-chapter ten lands the chapter''s central identity — *unity = the First Intellect = the Word = the cause = creativity = the Creator*. Sub-chapter eleven presses the impossibilities against *unity as other than the Intellect*. Sub-chapter twelve corrects the *forms became eternal* doctrine (valid only of the acquired intellects that unite with the *paradoxical intellect called the proximate sovereign, nicknamed the Pen* — *al-Qalam*). Sub-chapter thirteen corrects the *Intellect became like the Word* doctrine (the First Intellect IS the Word, not LIKE the Word). Sub-chapter fourteen refuses applying the name *Creator* to the intellect (shirk). Sub-chapters fifteen and sixteen refuse the *Intellect-as-intermediary* and *creativity-as-intermediary* doctrines and land the chapter''s centerpiece — *creativity IS the Creator* — anchored to Q 6:103 and Q 57:3, and crowned by the Imam Ali aphorism from *Nahj al-Balagha* on *whoever describes Him has limited Him*. The closing section settles the soteriological consequence: tawhid is *difficult*; Allah has from time to time singled out *one perfected man — the Imam, peace be upon him*; the *two worships* (zahir and batin) are the body and spirit of the religion; the chapter closes with the prayer for *Dar al-Quds*, *a noble station*, and the book closes with *Praise be to God, until He is praised as is due; and prayers upon Muhammad and his good family, all of them, the pure*. The book''s final chapter is its closing humility — the speaker who has worked the chain joint- by-joint stops at the last joint and *will not speak past it*.
