', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'kitab al riyad the perfect and the perfection of the soul', 'the perfect and the perfection of the soul the opening of kitab al riyad in full the damaged invocation that survives al kirmanis preface and prayer for the ruling imam his statement of method the table of ten chapters and the first ten sub chapters of chapter one where the foundational doctrine of the book perfect in itself deficient only in relation is established against al sijistanis persistent attempt to fix the soul on a single rank scale that would make it deficient in itself', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'kitab al riyad soul intellect and the power of emanation', 'soul intellect and the power of emanation the middle stretch of chapter one of kitab al riyad sub chapters eleven through twenty three where al sijistani exhausts his attacks on the perfection of the soul through a parade of analogies and al kirmani holds the formula perfect in itself deficient only in relation at every turn the two weighty things hadith arrives at the structural center the dolphins of the sea of oman arrive near the end the episode closes at the threshold of the sperm and man analogy that opens episode five', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'kitab al riyad the soul in time and the rejoinder to al nusra', 'the soul in time and the rejoinder to al nusra the closing stretch of chapter one of kitab al riyad sub chapters twenty four through thirty eight al sijistanis last lines of attack the sperm and man analogy the speaker and foundation ranking the male female pair the souls subject to nature that forget their world the fourfold division of objects idea words writing the intellect and time together claim meet al kirmanis decisive distinction between the universal soul and the sensual soul of struggling human beings the chapter ends on the prayer that seals the whole argument and on the bridge to chapter two', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'kitab al riyad the intellect as the first creation', 'the intellect as the first creation nine sub chapters of the second chapter of kitab al riyad al kirmanis narrow dense argument that the first and second intellects are not bodies and that the whole vocabulary of motion stillness need dwelling and encompassing therefore does not apply to them the chapter answers two competing wrong pictures al razis motion and stillness united in the first and the second and al sijistanis motion for the soul stillness for the intellect with the same correction the categories of bodies do not transfer to the world of origination the structural climax is the overflow doctrine the hudud withhold nothing the limitation is in the receivers capacity the quranic anchors 17 31 and 4 6 carry the doctrinal weight the chapter ends on the warning that confusion about the ranks of the hudud corrupts the essence of the souls that try to perceive without that discipline', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'kitab al riyad soul and spirit one substance or two', 'soul and spirit one substance or two six sub chapters of chapter three of kitab al riyad al kirmanis judgment among the hudud on whether the soul the second and the lower soul al hayuli prime matter are similar to the first or not the chapter answers the first and the second are like counterparts the mineral of honor perfection and elevation al hayuli is the trace of the second not its child the higher and lower worlds do not need to resemble each other to be in contact because the contact is the work of the power learned from the first running through the second impressing the substrate al nusras body categories sides distance diminution are refused one by one the closing sub chapter accepts al nusras final reconstruction of the chain intellect soul nature once he has dropped the body talk the doctrinal payload sets the architecture for the rest of al riyad the hudud are linked to their principles by office not by likeness', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'kitab al riyad souls parts of the first truths or only traces', 'souls parts of the first truths or only traces eight sub chapters of chapter four of kitab al riyad al kirmanis adjudication of the longest running disagreement between the two reformers are the speaking souls in us parts of the first truths the universal second or only traces of them the chapter walks the dispute through eight passes sub chapter one reads both reformers as half right al islahs traces protected the soul from body status al nusras parts protected the souls continuity with what stands above sub chapters two and three set up and refuse al nusras parts of the total soul reading by a precise dilemma sub chapter four delivers the doctrinal hinge the speaking souls are the second emanation the second source sub chapters five and six work the unlimited capacity proof of non embodiment and grant only a restricted parts by rank language sub chapter seven is the polemical climax prophethood imamate walaya and the divine laws do not stand or fall by the parts claim at all they rest on the rank attained by the prophets soul through the chain of obedience and worship on the hudud that convey the gift sub chapter eight delivers the deepest move the intellect is nothing but the word the word is nothing but the intellect the formula imam al muizz li din allah taught in tawil al sharia the doctrinal payload sets the architecture for the rest of al riyad the hudud convey the souls receive the chain holds by the running of the single originating power not by any substance composition between ranks', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'kitab al riyad the human being fruit of all the worlds', 'the human being fruit of all the worlds seven sub chapters of chapter five of kitab al riyad al kirmanis adjudication of the next great dispute between the two reformers is the human being the fruit of this world the harvest of the long natural ascent from elements into the form prepared to receive the three souls as the author of al islah said or the fruit of the higher world the abode of survival to which the prophets called us as the author of al nusra insisted sub chapter one frames the dispute and lands the third word the human being is the fruit of all the worlds first perfection from the natural world second perfection from the higher world on the authority of imam al muizz li din allahs tawil al sharia sub chapter two turns al nusras would not move objection back on him and gives the structural formula human beings are formed by the meeting of the overflow of the worlds sub chapter three refuses al islahs extension of emanation to al hayula and al nusras perfect and deficient marriage analogy settling the doctrine that each rank is perfect in itself and deficient only when added to another sub chapter four settles the name al tamamiyyah on the creator sub chapter five settles the synonymy of the divine names cause effect perfect perfected according to the additions one named multiple aspects the same architecture chapter four settled for intellect and word sub chapter six delivers the chapters deepest move the universal soul did not seek in her action god enriched her at origination her overflow is the work of giving not of getting from that the chapters signature formula human beings have two sides one part natural one part divine the last in the universes united in eternity with what has been acquired by time sub chapter seven closes on the first creators metaphorical action and the intellect that perceives only the truth the doctrinal payload sets the architecture for chapter sixs coming question of motion stillness al hayula and form the human being is the rank at which the chain of emanation from above meets the chain of natural cause and effect from below the hudud convey the gift the believer who knows the houses of the prophets the guardians the imams and the righteous is ready for salvation gathered in dar al quds', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'kitab al riyad motion stillness al hayula and form', 'motion stillness al hayula and form nine sub chapters of chapter six of kitab al riyad al kirmanis adjudication of the next argument the two reformers handed down what are motion stillness al hayula prime matter and form and what kind of analogy from soul and body if any can honestly capture them the author of al islah had offered a gentler and denser proportion motion and stillness as the soul of al hayula and form the author of al nusra had refused the proportion and offered a structural parallel keyed to division and similarity of parts sub chapter one frames the dispute and lands the chapters structural doctrine motion and stillness are opposites that do not meet in the same place at the same time form and al hayula are inseparable correlates actor and acted upon sub chapter two presses the consequence al islahs proportion would force the soul to be a body which it is not sub chapter three handles al nusras structural parallel and settles the synonymy of al nafs and al ruh as one non bodily reality sub chapter four delivers the positive doctrine of motion that by which the same thing changes and the architectural map form and al hayula as the first emanation sub chapter five handles al islahs analogy from kun and refuses it as an image sub chapter six handles the related doctrine of two proportions and qualifies the analogy by first and accident sub chapter seven is the chapters hinge the grave error of pre creative creativity refused categorically in the name of tawhid the creative is the creativity sub chapter eight seals the doctrine at the first effect above it no motion no rest no body talk whatsoever sub chapter nine closes on what contradictions among bodies actually are not in their being bodies but in the images within them the doctrinal payload sets the architecture for chapter sevens coming question of the sections of the world the bodys differentiation is at the form level the image level what al hayula has received the worlds architectural divisions follow the proportions of receivership at each rank the same architecture chapter four settled for the souls and chapter five settled for the human being', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'kitab al riyad the sections of the world', 'the sections of the world seven sub chapters of chapter seven of kitab al riyad al kirmanis adjudication of the sections of the world what the worlds actual divisions are where the human being sits among those sections what kind of pair the human has and on what footing the doctrine of the microcosm can honestly stand the author of al islah had offered a clean four jewels four structures four classes scheme with the horse paired to humankind by essence sharing and humankind itself as the counterpart of the whole world with no peer among its parts the author of al nusra had refused the four classes division and offered a pairing by acceptance scheme the dense lower world as pair of the subtle upper world the horse as humankinds pair because the horse accepts teaching from humankind more than other animals do sub chapter one frames the dispute and lands the architectural correction the world has many more sections than four the generated beings are three genera plus human by distinction sub chapter two refuses the no peer claim from two angles and lands the pairing by occasion doctrine angels lions wolves crows tigers monkeys depending on what the human has chosen to resemble sub chapter three settles the strict pair of strict sense humankind as the angels and names the walaya chain that constitutes the strict sense humankind sub chapter four turns to bandhaqlis empedocles on the bestial soul and speaking soul refuses al nusra s literal reading and gives the tawil teachers and receivers not horse and human with q 89 27 30 as the quranic anchor sub chapter five settles the architectural map of the microcosm in its disputed form al islah s body to bodies growth to plants sense to animals speaking soul as unique fourth preferred to al nusra s re mapping sub chapter six fills the map at full breadth and adds the creative emanative world parallels intellect praise tawhid virtue pointing the reader to rahat al aql sub chapter seven lands the between state doctrine the human is the child of both worlds matter from below and image from above the parents in the highest picture being the universal intellect and the universal soul the midwife who delivers being the hudud of the dawa the doctrinal payload sets the architecture for chapter eight s coming question of al qada and al qadar judgment and destiny how the chain runs in time', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'kitab al riyad qada and qadar fate and destiny', 'qada and qadar fate and destiny twenty four sub chapters of chapter eight of kitab al riyad al kirmanis adjudication of al qada judgment decree and al qadar destiny measure what they mean which is prior which is posterior and what the order of the chain that runs in time looks like at every rank the author of al islah had argued that destiny precedes decree because destiny is estimation and decree is detail like a tailor estimating a garment before he cuts it the author of al nusra had inverted the order decree precedes destiny like the spiritual image of the sewing in the tailors soul precedes the appreciation by which he prepares to cut sub chapters one and two frame the opening dispute on the definitions and the two tailor analogies and land al kirmanis first move the dispute cannot stand on the bodily reading alone sub chapters three through five give the three meanings of qada the speaker prophet the bringing out by tawil the metaphysical fixity of the worlds laws sub chapters six through eight give the three meanings of qadar the promised qaim the revelation and shariah the world unfolding in time with quran 70 6 7 as the quranic anchor sub chapter nine corrects the doctrine of the first intellect as the assembly of the images of the worlds sub chapters ten through fifteen work quran 54 49 indeed we have created everything according to a measure and give the pen tablet writer architecture sub chapters sixteen through nineteen work the prophetic word flee from allahs qada to his qadar in three meanings with quran 18 77 as al islahs anchor sub chapter twenty gives the political tawil of moses asking shuayb for the agency to lift the affliction of the people of truth at the wrong time quran 7 143 and quran 41 53 sub chapters twenty one and twenty two refuse the doctrine the mind is all things and all things are the mind sub chapter twenty three refuses al nusra s reading of quran 5 93 as a license to abandon the shariah and gives the verse a different tawil a license to the duat to study outside sciences as a weapon under three conditions sub chapter twenty four lands the three position order decree at the beginning apportioning in the middle completion at the end anchored to quran 89 27 30 and the reassured soul that returns to its lord pleased and pleasing the doctrinal payload sets the architecture for chapter nine s coming question of the law of adam and the wilayah of nuh how the chain that runs in time first establishes itself at the head of human history', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'kitab al riyad the shariah of adam and the first speaker', 'the shariah of adam and the first speaker the first nineteen sub chapters of chapter nine of kitab al riyad al kirmanis adjudication of whether adam peace be upon him had a shariah the author of al nusra presses that he did not the qurban offering verse is a parable one of adams sons did not know how to bury his brother until allah sent a raven the verse of shariah quran 42 13 names noah abraham moses jesus and muhammad ﷺ but not adam the angels are not assigned and are not obligated and if adam had been the owner of a shariah allah would have commanded the angels to prostrate themselves through him the author of al islah had affirmed the opposite the qurban settles it on its apparent reading the universally affirmative verses settle it independently and the speaker cannot be a speaker without a shariah drawn among his nation al kirmani works the dispute point by point the qurban on apparent the deep tawil of the two sons as wali and opposite and the raven as muhammad ibn abi bakr the narrative tradition naming adam as prescriber of the rites of the dead the prostration of the angels establishing adams mustafa rank the parallel verse to israel showing that silent intermediaries are not no intermediaries the natural world angels distinguished from the abode of eternity high limits the roster argument refuted by the parallel roster of quran 4 163 the three souls and the six gates of necessity constitutive of human being as such the noah parallel showing violation presupposes prior limits the tent at the qibla and seth as first asas the refusal of the bare world sufficient for tawhid doctrine the shariah of adam peace be upon him is fixed the chapter bridges to its own second half the prophets as teachers doctrine the monotheism and the ranks architecture and the wilayah of noah', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'kitab al riyad prophets as teachers monotheism and the ranks', 'prophets as teachers monotheism and the ranks the second half of chapter nine of kitab al riyad fourteen sub chapters in which al kirmani takes the verdict of the first half adam had a shariah and seth was his asas and expands it into the architectural shape of the chain that runs through human history sub chapter twenty the work is for soul correction not only for tawhid the deep reading of i know what you do not know quran 2 30 on the architecture of force and act across the cycles sub chapters twenty one and twenty two distinguishing the interregnum role of the qaim knowledge widespread from the post qaim role door of education closed sub chapter twenty three the three sections of adams call encouragement intimidation or both each requiring parable and therefore apparent meaning followed by interpretation sub chapter twenty four knowledge and action together as the one shariah body and spirit of the religion sub chapter twenty five the qaim completes does not abrogate the apocalypse verses concern the passing of the ranks not the laws and ordinances of works sub chapter twenty six the high limits attain perfection from innovation the bodily intellects from acquisition through teaching sub chapters twenty seven and twenty eight the seven lines and six aradas dispute al islahs reading corrected al nusras refused al kirmanis positive geometry of the speaker cycles sub chapter twenty nine as we began the first creation we shall repeat it quran 21 104 on the cyclic return sub chapter thirty the adam letters argument refuted by internal inconsistency sub chapters thirty one through thirty three the ark of noah with both readings sound apparent shariah dawa with the wali the doctrine that every speaker has a guardian adam had seth and the deep tawil of the son of noah as the opposite who is washed away in the cleansing flood that spreads outwardly and inwardly the type that recurs at every cycle until the seventh bridge to chapter ten tawhid and the critique of al mahsul the doctrine of the first creator above the head of the chain', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'kitab al riyad tawhid and the critique of al mahsul', 'tawhid and the critique of al mahsul sixteen sub chapters of chapter ten of kitab al riyad the books closing critique al kirmani turns from the architecture of the chain to the rank above the chain tawhid taw heed monotheism and al mubdi al awwal the first creator the author of al mahsul had written a chapter on the first thing in what was first to fix tawhid and the first creator abu hatim al razi in al islah had passed over the errors al kirmani enters at the close of his own treatise having promised the work at the books beginning to discharge the right of religion by stating what is implicated in al mahsuls chapter and what it is not permissible to believe sub chapter one frames the doctrine of cooperation in the sciences of religion like the senses sub chapter two lands the threefold impossibility against the creator of the thing and what is not under these elements sub chapters three through eight work the grammar of nothing and refuse al mahsuls he and no thing with him with the verse of self witness q 3 18 the verse of incomparability q 42 11 and the cairo court doctrine of huwa as the only pronoun positive for what is outside of it without any attribute or plurality anchored to imam al muizz li din allahs tawil al sharai sub chapter nine corrects al mahsuls confusion of the acquired intellect which gathers the images of both worlds with the first intellect which is self knowing alone sub chapter ten lands the chapters central identity unity the first intellect the word the cause creativity the creator sub chapter eleven presses the impossibilities against unity as other than the intellect sub chapter twelve corrects the forms became eternal doctrine valid only of the acquired intellects that unite with the paradoxical intellect called the proximate sovereign nicknamed the pen al qalam sub chapter thirteen corrects the intellect became like the word doctrine the first intellect is the word not like the word sub chapter fourteen refuses applying the name creator to the intellect shirk sub chapters fifteen and sixteen refuse the intellect as intermediary and creativity as intermediary doctrines and land the chapters centerpiece creativity is the creator anchored to q 6 103 and q 57 3 and crowned by the imam ali aphorism from nahj al balagha on whoever describes him has limited him the closing section settles the soteriological consequence tawhid is difficult allah has from time to time singled out one perfected man the imam peace be upon him the two worships zahir and batin are the body and spirit of the religion the chapter closes with the prayer for dar al quds a noble station and the book closes with praise be to god until he is praised as is due and prayers upon muhammad and his good family all of them the pure the books final chapter is its closing humility the speaker who has worked the chain joint by joint stops at the last joint and will not speak past it', '');
